3|0@SmartThings^1440187233#cub1nex:drbudro$:You should check out [HomeSeer](http://www.homeseer.com/compare-home-controllers.html). It was a little more than I wanted to spend on home automation, but for under $1k you can have a completely offline solution. I don't think they support Zigbee or bluetooth 4, so if you're heavily invested in those protocols, it might price you out. OpenHAB was another one I considered briefly, but I have enough homebrew projects that take enough of my time to maintain. It may be worth it for you since you can essentially get anything you want out of it and it's completely off-net. There are also 3rd party solutions like [MySensors](http://www.mysensors.org/about/arduino) that take existing hubs and give them the ability to integrate with additional devices. My HVAC and irrigation systems are being ported over to an arduino controlled system that uses only my home WiFi for connectivity, so I'll have the ability to integrate that with the rest of my home security/automation. They have online portals so I can control them from outside the home, but do all the computing w/o the Internet.
1|0@SmartThings^1440189802#cub3ag5:[deleted]$:I did try homeseer, but it takes over $1300 in unsupported, 3rd party software to get my current Z-wave, ZigBee and Hue devices joined under one interface. I like the idea of OpenHAB, but same thing as you, not enough time to run a Pi, hack a Wink, and maintain my own codebase. Mysensors is even worse, you have to assemble your own hub and add hardware modules to each new technology. The whole point of the ZigBee protocol was to replace X-10 in such a way as so the *Average* consumer could get a system up and running for under $500. I have almost $2K in compatible hardware for Z-Wave, Wifi and ZigBee, but the only hubs that will work out of the box have no compute of their own until you get to rack-mounted $18,000 and up systems (Savant mostly). I just want a processor **in** the hub. I'd pay $200 more for it, even if it were no more than a Raspberry Pi, as long as it meant I could run my house without needing to give up my data or be constantly dependant on an outside party.
